    {
      "item_id": "36c50723-3b65-49f6-9d9b-70574cc9471c",
      "session_id": "AdditionalQuestions-36c50723-what-happened-to-salander-in-the-abandoned-fa",
      "trace_id": "fc613fc9f09580203ee2ee3aff72d62a",
      "output": {
        "question": "What happened to Salander in the abandoned factory?",
        "answer": "In the abandoned brickworks (factory), Lisbeth Salander was shot three times by Alexander Zalachenko after he appeared and fired a Browning .22 calibre pistol [E1]. The first bullet hit her hip, causing her left leg to drag [E2]. The second bullet struck her shoulder, paralyzing her left arm [E2]. The third bullet penetrated her skull about an inch below the top of her left ear, causing massive trauma and making her lose consciousness [E1] . She discovered the wound in her skull after regaining enough consciousness to check herself, realizing she was touching her own brain [E3]. Overcome by weariness from these injuries, she lay down on a kitchen bench [E3].",
        "citations": [
          "[E1]",
          "[E2]",
          "[E3]"
        ],
        "reranked": [
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_072.html",
            "paragraph": 160,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "140",
            "text": "Zalachenko had time to aim.\nThe third bullet caught her about an inch below the top of her left ear. It penetrated her skull and caused a spiderweb of radial cracks in her cranium. The lead came to rest in the grey matter about two inches beneath the cerebral cortex, by the cerebrum.\nFor Salander the medical detail was academic. The bullet caused immediate massive trauma. Her last sensation was a glowing red shock that turned into a white light.",
            "paragraph_end": 162,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "95082038310d0f20d644b16ea7354b422afd424f6c1d5b8a56188d91805efb4d",
            "id": "95082038-310d-0f20-d644-b16ea7354b42",
            "agent_label": "E1"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_074.html",
            "paragraph": 119,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "144",
            "text": "She went closer to the mirror and was suddenly aware that her left leg was dragging behind her. She had a sharp pain in her hip where Zalachenko’s first bullet had hit her. His second bullet had struck her shoulder and paralyzed her left arm. It hurt.\nBut the pain in her head was so sharp it made her stagger. Slowly she raised her right hand and fumbled across the back of her head. With her fingers she could feel the crater of the entry wound.",
            "paragraph_end": 120,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "4faf54eea378880bb6ea2fa865707300be4c9108600dcd7d32db14062a705079",
            "id": "4faf54ee-a378-880b-b6ea-2fa865707300",
            "agent_label": "E2"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Played with Fire",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Played with Fire _ A Lisbeth Salander Novel -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium trilogy, bk_ 2, New York, 2009 -- Alfred A_ Knopf -- isbn13 9780307272300 -- 078fc916144df1e3707c069bbf984d06 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_who-ayed_with_Fire_split_074.html",
            "paragraph": 121,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": null,
            "page": "144",
            "text": "As she fingered the hole in her skull she realized with sudden horror that she was touching her own brain, that she was so seriously wounded she was dying or maybe should already be dead. She couldn’t comprehend how she could still be on her feet.\nShe was suddenly overcome by a numbing weariness. She wasn’t sure if she was about to faint or fall asleep, but she made her way to the kitchen bench, where she stretched out and laid the unwounded right side of her head on a cushion.",
            "paragraph_end": 122,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
